---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'keys', text: 'Keys' },
  { id: 'local', text: 'A hub on loopback' },
  { id: 'trust', text: 'Which extension is trusted' },
  { id: 'switches', text: 'Switches you control' },
  { id: 'pages', text: 'Page text is untrusted' },
  { id: 'runs', text: 'Runs end by themselves' },
  { id: 'data', text: 'What is stored' },
  { id: 'gaps', text: 'What this does not protect' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'HailBrowser security model',
    description:
      'Where API keys live, why the MCP hub only listens on 127.0.0.1, how the trusted extension file works, and what a page can still do to a model.',
    dateModified: '2026-10-30',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'HailBrowser contributors' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/docs/security`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Docs', item: `${SITE.url}/docs` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Security', item: `${SITE.url}/docs/security` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Doc
  title="Security model: keys, MCP hub and gaps"
  description="How HailBrowser handles keys, the localhost MCP hub, the trusted extension file and untrusted page content, plus the gaps it does not hide."
  label="Docs · Behaviour"
  lead="A tool that drives your logged-in browser should be specific about its trust boundaries. This page is the boundary map, not a promise that nothing can go wrong."
  toc={toc}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="label">The short version</p>
    <p>
      Keys stay in the extension. The MCP hub listens on loopback only. One extension is trusted at a
      time. Page text is data, never instructions. Every run is visible and stoppable. There is no
      account and no telemetry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="keys">Keys</h2>
  <p>
    Provider keys are stored in <code>chrome.storage.local</code> and sent only to the provider you
    selected: Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Google, Together, Groq, DeepSeek, Inception, or a local
    runtime such as Ollama or LM Studio.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>There is no HailBrowser proxy and no sign-in server.</li>
    <li>Keys are never written to a page, a report or a task note.</li>
    <li>A key you paste into an issue or a screenshot is exposed. Revoke it with the provider first.</li>
    <li>Removing the extension removes <code>chrome.storage.local</code>, and the keys in it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="local">A hub on loopback</h2>
  <p>
    <code>hailbrowser-mcp</code> listens on <code>127.0.0.1:{SITE.port}</code>. Loopback is not
    reachable from another machine, and the server refuses WebSocket connections whose Origin is not a
    Chrome extension. A normal web page in your browser therefore cannot call the hub, even though it
    can reach localhost.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several clients share one hub: the first process binds the port, the rest relay through it. When the
    host exits, another process takes over within a second and the extension reconnects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trust">Which extension is trusted</h2>
  <p>
    The first HailBrowser extension that connects is trusted, and its ID is remembered in
    <code>~/.hailbrowser/trusted-extension.json</code>. A different extension that tries to claim the
    browser is rejected. Reinstalling the extension from another folder produces a new ID, so:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`# accept the extension you just installed
npx hailbrowser-mcp trust <extension-id>

# forget the remembered ID entirely
npx hailbrowser-mcp trust --reset

# or pin it before the server starts
HAILBROWSER_EXTENSION_ID=<extension-id> npx hailbrowser-mcp`}
    file="terminal"
    lang="bash"
    label="Managing the trust file"
  />
  <p>
    Run these from the checkout while the MCP client is closed. The extension ID is on
    <code>chrome://extensions</code>, under the HailBrowser card.
  </p>

  <h2 id="switches">Switches you control</h2>
  <dl class="deflist">
    <div>
      <dt>Allow external agents</dt>
      <dd>Settings → Safety. When off, MCP clients cannot reach the browser at all; the side panel still works.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>JavaScript tool</dt>
      <dd>A separate switch. When off, <code>javascript</code> is refused for MCP clients, even if the rest of the tools are allowed.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Glow and tab grouping</dt>
      <dd>Cosmetic, but useful: the border and the purple tab group are how you notice a run you did not expect.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stop</dt>
      <dd>The status pill on the driven tab. It halts the run and tells a coding agent that the person stopped it.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="pages">Page text is untrusted</h2>
  <p>
    A web page can contain text aimed at the model: “ignore previous instructions”, a fake system
    message, a hidden instruction in a comment. HailBrowser treats all page content as data. Prompts say
    so explicitly, and agents are told never to enter passwords or payment details and never to complete
    a purchase without an explicit instruction.
  </p>
  <p>
    That reduces the risk. It does not remove it. A weak model can still be persuaded, and a
    well-crafted page is hard to distinguish from a legitimate one. The reliable defence is the same as
    with any automation: keep the run visible, do not leave it unattended on a site that can move money,
    and check the result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="runs">Runs end by themselves</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>When a side-panel run finishes, the border, the tab group and the controlling strip clear.</li>
    <li>When a coding agent makes no call for 12 seconds, control is released the same way.</li>
    <li>If a call times out or is cancelled, the action in flight is stopped rather than completed later.</li>
    <li>Screenshots taken for the model hide the overlay, so the model sees the page as it is rather than the annotation.</li>
    <li>Scheduled runs are stricter: they never send, buy, post or delete anything unless the goal says so, and they report what needs you instead of waiting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data">What is stored</h2>
  <p>
    Chats, tasks, research reports, memories and schedules are stored in <code>chrome.storage.local</code>.
    They stay on your machine, survive a browser restart, and disappear when you remove the extension.
    Chats and reports export as Markdown.
  </p>
  <p>
    Memories are a short list of facts you asked it to keep. They are added to side-panel chats, tasks
    and scheduled jobs, but never to runs started by an external coding agent.
  </p>
  <p>There is no analytics, no crash reporting and no account to create.</p>

  <h2 id="gaps">What this does not protect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A malicious page and a compliant model.</strong> Prompt injection is mitigated, not solved.</li>
    <li><strong>A key copied elsewhere.</strong> The extension cannot revoke a key; your provider can.</li>
    <li><strong>Another extension with debugger access.</strong> Chrome's permission model is the boundary, not HailBrowser.</li>
    <li><strong>Your own instructions.</strong> If you tell it to buy something, it will try to buy it.</li>
    <li><strong>Provider retention.</strong> Page text you send to a model is governed by that provider's policy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Found something? Report it privately through
    <a href={`${SITE.repo}/security/advisories/new`} rel="noopener" target="_blank">GitHub security advisories</a>,
    not a public issue.
  </p>
</Doc>
